      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Stage 1 of 2 \u2014 generate the art\n\n**This is the stage that costs money.** It ends by writing raw, unframed art to\n`tarot/derived-art/`, one file per card, named by slot: `00_the-fool.png`, `16_the-tower.png`.\nStage 2 picks that folder up.\n\n## Why this is split from the assembly\n\nGeneration is slow and paid; borders and lettering are instant and free. Keeping them in one\ngraph means every experiment with a keyline sits downstream of 78 API calls. Cut here and a\nborder is a stage-2 problem you can chew on all evening at no cost.\n\nThe result cache already makes a re-queue cheap, but only while the request is byte-identical\n\u2014 change one word of the style block and it is gone. **A file on disk is not.**\n\n## The handoff\n\nTwo things cross the boundary, and both are files:\n\n- **The art**, named `{index:02d}_{label}` so stage 2's Slot Loader maps it straight back onto\n  the right positions.\n- **The deck definition**, written to `tarot/deck.json` by `save_definition_to`. Stage 2 loads\n  that same file rather than re-declaring the deck, so the two stages cannot drift apart on\n  names, order or scope. If you rename a card here, stage 2 letters the new name.\n\n`save_sidecar_json` is on, so each card lands beside a JSON of the prompt that made it.\n\n## Before you run\n\n`scope` is on **proof set** \u2014 twelve cards that exercise every scoping dimension. Read the\nResolve report, and only then switch to `full deck`."
      ],
      "title": "Read me \u2014 stage 1 generates",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
